<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2"><Document><name>Gauss's great triangle: Hohehagen, Brocken, Inselsberg</name><description>Gauss's largest measured triangle. Observed angles: Hohehagen 86° 13′ 58.366″, Brocken 53° 6′ 45.642″, Inselsberg 40° 39′ 30.165″. Sum 180° 0′ 14.173″. Computed spherical excess 14.853″. Closure −0.680″ (Werke IX, p. 300). Disquisitiones generales, art. 28: excess 14.85348″, reductions Hohehagen −4.95113″, Brocken −4.95104″, Inselsberg −4.95131″ (Werke IV, p. 258). Area from the printed positions: 2,934 km². A plane triangle sums to exactly 180°. Sources: Gauss, C. F. (1903). Werke IX, p. 300 (angles, sum, excess, closure) and pp. 427-428 (positions); Gauss, C. F. (1873). Werke IV, p. 258 (reductions). Göttingen meridian placed at 9° 56′ 35″ E.</description><Style id="c"><IconStyle><scale>1.1</scale><Icon><href>http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/red-circle.png</href></Icon></IconStyle><LabelStyle><scale>1.0</scale></LabelStyle></Style><Style id="d"><IconStyle><scale>0</scale></IconStyle><LabelStyle><color>ff00ffff</color><scale>1.0</scale></LabelStyle></Style><Style id="s"><LineStyle><color>ff1010e0</color><width>4</width></LineStyle></Style><Style id="t"><LineStyle><color>ff1010e0</color><width>4</width></LineStyle><PolyStyle><color>401010e0</color></PolyStyle></Style>
